    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})^2=x+y+2\\sqrt{xy}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})^2=x+y+2\\sqrt{xy}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})^2&=(\\sqrt{x})^2+2\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}+(\\sqrt{y})^2\\\\(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})^2&=x+2\\sqrt{xy}+y\\\\(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})^2&=x+y+2\\sqrt{xy}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-b",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Use this result to find:",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p24-q1-b-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "$\\sqrt{16+2\\sqrt{55}}$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$\\sqrt{11}+\\sqrt{5}$.\n\nUse $11+5=16$ and $11\\times5=55$ in part a.",
              "worked": "Since $11+5=16$ and $11\\times5=55$, part a gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{16+2\\sqrt{55}}&=\\sqrt{11+5+2\\sqrt{11\\times5}}\\\\\\sqrt{16+2\\sqrt{55}}&=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{11}+\\sqrt{5})^2}\\\\\\sqrt{16+2\\sqrt{55}}&=\\sqrt{11}+\\sqrt{5}\\end{align*}$$\nThe sum is positive, so it is the principal square root.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 32
          },
          {
            "id": "p24-q1-b-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "$\\sqrt{16-2\\sqrt{55}}$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$\\sqrt{11}-\\sqrt{5}$.\n\nUse the corresponding square-of-a-difference identity and take the positive root.",
              "worked": "The corresponding difference identity gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\sqrt{11}-\\sqrt{5})^2&=11+5-2\\sqrt{11\\times5}\\\\(\\sqrt{11}-\\sqrt{5})^2&=16-2\\sqrt{55}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\sqrt{11}>\\sqrt{5}$, the difference is positive. Hence\n$$\\sqrt{16-2\\sqrt{55}}=\\sqrt{11}-\\sqrt{5}.$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 40
          },
          {
            "id": "p24-q1-b-iii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iii",
            "prompt": "$\\sqrt{11+2\\sqrt{30}}$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{5}$.\n\nUse $6+5=11$ and $6\\times5=30$ in part a.",
              "worked": "Since $6+5=11$ and $6\\times5=30$, part a gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{11+2\\sqrt{30}}&=\\sqrt{6+5+2\\sqrt{6\\times5}}\\\\\\sqrt{11+2\\sqrt{30}}&=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{5})^2}\\\\\\sqrt{11+2\\sqrt{30}}&=\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{5}\\end{align*}$$\nThe sum is positive, so it is the principal square root.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 32
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
